## E. AI CHECK IMAGE

For an attached image, provide a separate AI CHECK IMAGE action.

Use the same FreeLLM-compatible API endpoint and authentication method described above.

For image analysis, use the OpenAI-compatible multimodal request format demonstrated by my working example.

The user message must contain:
- A concise image-review prompt.
- An image_url content part containing a valid base64 data URI, for example:

{
  "type": "image_url",
  "image_url": {
    "url": "data:image/jpeg;base64,ACTUAL_BASE64_IMAGE_DATA"
  }
}

The image MIME type and data URI must match the actual uploaded image. Do not label every image as JPEG regardless of its format.

The prompt must include the post title, caption/details, selected platform, role, and applicable starter guidelines.

Use this analysis instruction:

"You are BrandLens, an image content assistant. Analyze the supplied image for the proposed social media or marketing post. Judge only what is visible in the image and the supplied post context. Explain in one or two simple sentences what the image communicates and whether it looks suitable for this post. Give one short, practical improvement if needed; if the image already looks suitable, suggest one optional improvement. Do not invent details, brand assets, text, or facts that are not visible. Do not produce lengthy explanations, scores, hashtags, or extra sections."

Ask the API for a compact structured response:

{
  "verdict": "looks_good | changes_suggested | needs_attention",
  "briefAnalysis": "One or two sentences",
  "suggestion": "One short practical improvement",
  "issues": []
}

Display the image preview alongside the result when appropriate.

The user should be able to run image review separately from text review. One review must not overwrite the other.

If a file is not an image, disable the image review action and explain that image review currently supports images only.

If the API returns an error, timeout, rate limit, or unreadable result, show a helpful error and preserve the existing post form contents.

## F. GENERATE IMAGE — CONNECT MY IMAGE GENERATOR

Add a GENERATE IMAGE button to the New Post page.

Use this two-step pipeline:

STEP 1 — Generate an image prompt using the same FreeLLM-compatible API.

Send:
- Title.
- Caption/details.
- Selected platform.
- Current user's role.
