# 6. BACKEND API AND SECRETS

The FreeLLM and image-generation APIs must be called from the server-side integration layer, not directly from browser JavaScript.

Use the existing backend architecture and route conventions wherever possible.

If the current application already has a backend, add the required endpoints to it without replacing the existing server.

If there is no backend, introduce the smallest compatible server-side API layer supported by the existing project. Do not migrate to a different framework just for convenience.

Create or reuse routes equivalent to:

POST /api/ai/check-text
POST /api/ai/check-image
POST /api/ai/generate-image
POST /api/posts
GET /api/posts
GET /api/posts/:id
PUT /api/posts/:id
GET /api/posts/:id/activity

Adapt route names as necessary to the existing architecture, but keep the responsibilities separated.

Required environment configuration:

FREELLM_API_KEY=replace_with_your_rotated_key
FREELLM_API_URL=https://invalid-ayush-llm.onrender.com/v1/chat/completions
IMAGE_GEN_URL=https://ai-image.ayushbhai1818.workers.dev/
IMAGE_GEN_KEY=invalid
MAX_UPLOAD_MB=25

These are example configuration entries, not actual credentials. The application's server must read the environment variables at runtime.

Requirements:
- Create a safe .env.example containing placeholders, never the real FreeLLM API key.
- Ensure .env and uploaded secret/config files are excluded by .gitignore.
- Never hardcode API keys into frontend files.
- Never print Authorization headers or keys into logs.
- Never return raw provider errors that could reveal credentials.
- Validate requests and uploads.
- Use explicit request timeouts and clear error handling.
- Handle HTTP 401/403, 429, 5xx errors, timeouts, invalid JSON, missing choices, and unsupported responses gracefully.
- Avoid unlimited retries or repeated image-generation calls.
- The interface must remain usable when an AI provider is temporarily unavailable.

For an image-analysis request, read the actual server-side uploaded image, convert the bytes to base64, build the correct MIME-specific data URI, and send it through the supported image_url message structure. Do not use a local file path in a remote API request.

If the existing execution environment genuinely cannot support the needed secure server-side calls, explain that limitation and implement no insecure client-side API-key workaround.

